@page "/"
@* The landing page shows no client data, so it is open to anyone. It says what the site does here:
   on the demo every step works; elsewhere, until sign-in exists, no campaign can be opened. It is
   headed with the caller's client by the name its people know it by, as the layout draws that
   client's look: neither is client data. With no client (signed out, the operator, or several), it
   names nobody. *@
@attribute [AllowAnonymous]
@inject SiteMode Site
@inject ClientWorkspace Workspace
@inject ILogger<Home> Log

<PageTitle>@_heading</PageTitle>

<h1 data-testid="home-heading">@_heading</h1>

@if (Site.IsDemo)
{
    <p>
        Write a campaign from one of your templates, see the checks as you go, approve it, and copy it
        into Square. Start in <a href="campaigns">Campaigns</a>, or keep your photos in
        <a href="images">Images</a> and the offers you write again and again in <a href="known-items">Known items</a>.
    </p>
}
else
{
    <p>Sign-in is not set up yet, so this app does not show or change any campaigns.</p>
}

<p><a href="how-it-works">How a campaign gets from idea to inbox</a></p>

@code {
    private string _heading = "Campaigns";

    // A name that cannot be read is logged and the page goes on naming nobody: the landing page
    // never stops for it.
    protected override async Task OnInitializedAsync()
    {
        var (client, _) = await Workspace.LookClientAsync();
        if (client is null) return;
        try
        {
            _heading = $"{await Workspace.DisplayNameAsync(client)} campaigns";
        }
        catch (Exception ex) when (ex is not OperationCanceledException)
        {
            Log.LogError(ex, "The display name of {Client} could not be read, so the landing page names no client.", client.Value);
        }
    }
}
